<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/inventions?focus=hydroelectricity">Inventions &amp; Discoveries</a> · Energy and power</p><h1 class="enc-title">Hydroelectricity</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1878">1878</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as hydroelectric power</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Hydroelectricity is electricity generated from hydropower, the energy of moving water. Water had long driven machinery, but the first hydroelectric power scheme was developed in 1878 by William Armstrong at Cragside in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/northumberland">Northumberland</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>, where it powered a single arc lamp in his art gallery. The Schoelkopf Power Station No. 1 near Niagara Falls began to produce electricity in 1881, and by 1889 there were 200 hydroelectric power stations in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> alone. Dams grew larger through the 20th century, to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/three-gorges">Three Gorges</a> Dam in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> at 22.5 GW, and hydropower supplied 15% of the world's electricity in 2023.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/hydroelectricity.jpg" alt="Hydroelectricity" width="330" height="205" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Source file : Le Grand Portage; derivative work by Rehman · CC BY 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:ThreeGorgesDam-China2009.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
